sources:
    {"organization":"Britannica","title":"Atomic Nucleus","url":"https://www.britannica.com/science/atomic-nucleus"}

definition:
    The positively charged core of an atom, made of protons and neutrons bound together by the strong nuclear force. It holds more than 99.9% of the atom's mass while spanning only about 1/100,000 of the atom's diameter — matter at this density would weigh hundreds of millions of tonnes per cubic centimeter. Ernest Rutherford discovered the nucleus in 1911 by firing alpha particles at gold foil and finding that a rare few bounced straight back.
